## 7 : CACHE-ASIDE PATTERN
==========================

Cache use karne ke different patterns hote hain.

Sabse commonly used pattern hai: Cache-Aside

Isko Lazy Loading bhi kaha jata hai.

Is pattern me:

Application pehle Cache check karti hai.
Agar data mil gaya → directly return.
Agar data nahi mila → Database se fetch.
Database se mila hua data Cache me store.
Phir response return.


---------
## FLOW:
---------

    GET /products/101
            ↓
      productId = 101
            ↓
    Application constructs the Cache Key
    using a predefined naming convention
            ↓
      "product:" + productId
            ↓
         product:101
            ↓
    Application checks the Cache using this key
            ↓
        Cache Hit?
        │
        ├── YES
        │     ↓
        │  Return Data
        │
        └── NO → Cache Miss
                  ↓
           Fetch Data from Database
                  ↓
           Application uses the same predefined
           naming convention to construct the Cache Key
                  ↓
           "product:" + productId
                ↓
           product:101
                ↓
           Store Data in Cache
                ↓
           Return Response


----------------
## IMPORTANT :
----------------

Cache-Aside me Cache implementation khud Database se data fetch nahi karta.
Cache Miss hone par Application Database se data fetch karti hai
aur retrieved data ko Cache me store karti hai before returning
the response.

Lekin kuch caching patterns / architectures me Cache implementation Cache Miss hone par Database se data fetch kar sakti hai.
Is concept ko broadly Read-Through Cache kehte hain.


====================
## 8 : STALE DATA
====================

Cache use karne ka ek important problem hai:

Database aur Cache ke beech data inconsistent ho sakta hai.


Suppose pehli baar request aati hai:

    GET /products/101

Cache me data available nahi hai → Cache Miss.

Application Database se data fetch karti hai aur Cache me
store kar deti hai:

    Database / Cache:

    product:101
    price = ₹79,999


Ab product ka price update hota hai:

    PUT /products/101

Database update ho jata hai:

    Database:

    product:101
    price = ₹69,999


Lekin Cache me purana data abhi bhi stored hai:

    Cache:

    product:101
    price = ₹79,999


Ab next request:

    GET /products/101

Cache Hit hone par user ko purana price ₹79,999 mil sakta hai.

Is purane / outdated cached data ko Stale Data kehte hain.


===========================
## 9 : CACHE INVALIDATION
===========================

Cache Invalidation ka simple meaning hai:

Jab Database me data change hota hai, to Cache me stored
corresponding outdated data ko remove ya update karna,
taaki application stale data serve na kare.

Database me data successfully update hone ke baad,
Application generally corresponding cache key ke saath
do approaches use kar sakti hai:


### 1. DELETE / INVALIDATE CACHE
-----------------------------------

Update Request
       |
       v
Update Data in Database
       |
       v
DEL product:101


Yaani corresponding cache entry ko delete kar diya jata hai.

Next read par:

Cache Miss
    ↓
Database se latest data fetch
    ↓
Cache me store
    ↓
Return Response


### 2. UPDATE CACHE
---------------------

Update Request
       |
       v
Update the cached data
corresponding to key `product:101`


Yaani existing cache key ke corresponding outdated data ko
latest data se replace kar diya jata hai.


## IMPORTANT
-------------

Cache-Aside pattern me commonly Database update ke baad
corresponding cache entry ko delete kiya jata hai.

Next read par Application Database se latest data fetch
karti hai aur Cache ko dobara populate kar deti hai.


=============
## 10 : TTL 
=============

TTL define karta hai ki:

Ek cache entry maximum kitne time tak available rahegi.

Maan lo hum Redis ko cache ke roop me use kar rahe hain.

Suppose humne `product:101` ko cache me store kiya.

Matlab Redis me conceptually:

Key        → product:101


Value      → {
                "id": 101,
                "name": "iPhone",
                "price": 79999
             }


TTL        → 10 minutes


Yahan TTL = 10 minutes ka matlab hai ki `product:101`
aur uski corresponding value cache me store hone ke baad
maximum 10 minutes tak available rahegi.


TTL expire hone ke baad:

    product:101
        +
    uski corresponding value

Cache me available nahi rahegi.


Agar uske baad same data ke liye request aati hai:

    GET /products/101
            ↓
       Cache Miss
            ↓
       Database
            ↓
      Latest Data
            ↓
    Store Again in Cache
            ↓
      New TTL Starts


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: AGAR DATABASE ME DATA UPDATE KARNE KE BAAD HUM CACHE KO INVALIDATE KAR RAHE HAIN, TO THEORETICALLY STALE DATA SERVE NAHI HONA CHAHIYE. PHIR TTL KI ZARURAT KYUN HAI?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TTL ek automatic expiration mechanism hai.

Ye ensure karta hai ki Cache entry indefinitely Cache me
na rahe.


## 1. CACHE INVALIDATION CAN FAIL
---------------------------------

Suppose Product data update hua:

    Update Request
          ↓
    Update Data in Database
          ↓
Application executes Cache
Invalidation Logic
          ↓
    DEL product:101


Cache invalidation execute karte time:

    - Redis unavailable ho sakta hai
    - Network failure ho sakta hai
    - Request timeout ho sakti hai
    - Application crash ho sakta hai


## RESULT:
-----------

Database successfully update ho gaya,
lekin Cache invalidation successfully nahi hui.


    Database:

    product:101
    price = ₹69,999


Lekin Cache me abhi bhi:

    product:101
    price = ₹79,999


Next request:

    GET /products/101
            ↓
        Cache Hit
            ↓
    Stale / Old Data


Agar Cache entry par TTL configured nahi hai, to user ko stale / outdated data milta rahega, 
aur ye stale cache entry potentially indefinitely Cache me stored reh sakti hai.


Lekin agar:

    TTL = 10 minutes


to even agar Cache invalidation fail ho jaye:

    Database Updated
          ↓
    Cache Invalidation Failed
          ↓
    Stale Data remains in Cache
          ↓
    TTL Eventually Expires
          ↓
    Cache Entry Unavailable
          ↓
    Next Request → Cache Miss
          ↓
    Fetch Latest Data from Database
          ↓
    Store Again in Cache


So:

TTL Cache Invalidation ka replacement nahi hai.

Cache Invalidation ka purpose data change hone par
corresponding stale cache entry ko immediately remove
karna hai.

TTL ek safety mechanism provide karta hai, taaki agar
Cache invalidation kisi reason se fail ho jaye, to stale
data indefinitely Cache me na rahe.



## 2. MEMORY MANAGEMENT
-------------------------

Cache RAM use karta hai, aur RAM unlimited nahi hoti.

Suppose application continuously different products cache kar rahi hai:

product:1
product:2
product:3
...
product:10,000,000


## Without TTL:
----------------

Ab maan lo inme se kai products ke liye future me kabhi koi read / GET request nahi aati, aur unka data bhi kabhi update nahi hota.

Is case me unki corresponding cache entries ko explicitly invalidate karne ka koi trigger nahi hoga, aur ye entries unnecessarily cache ki RAM occupy karti rahengi.


## TTL ke saath:
----------------

TTL expire hone ke baad cache entry automatically remove ho jaati hai, jisse woh unnecessarily RAM occupy nahi karti.


So, TTL helps ensure ki cache entries,
especially old / unused entries,
indefinitely cache ki RAM occupy na karein.


